% --- SIDEBAR: TLDR -----------------------------------------------------------
\sidebarlabel{TL;DR}
{\tldrfont An off-chip power meter is the one channel a verifier can read
without trusting the operator's silicon. It cannot count operations ---
cheating strategies hide 0.4 machines of covert compute behind an accepted
trace --- but it can detect the cadence of synchronous training, even
against an adaptive adversary.\par}

\sidebarrule

% --- SIDEBAR: CONTRIBUTIONS --------------------------------------------------
\sidebarlabel{Contributions}
\contribution{1}{A closed-form floor $\beta$ for the covert compute a power
trace cannot rule out, with every input measured on NVIDIA A100s.}
\contribution{2}{A capability ladder pricing what each added verifier
capability buys, from a bare meter ($\beta^{*}{=}1.95$) to re-execution
($0.34$).}
\contribution{3}{A tracked cyclostationary detector that recovers the
training cadence (AUC 1.00) and survives cheap de-periodicisation that
defeats every fixed-frequency test.}
\contribution{4}{Hard ceilings on the channel: a 1\,Hz integrating meter
defeats all detectors, and gradient-discarding decoys score as training
under every rule.}

