% --- TITLE BLOCK -------------------------------------------------------------
{\color{maintext}\titlefont What Can a Power Meter Certify About AI Compute?\par}
\vspace{0.3cm}
%{\color{subtitlegray}\subtitlefont Against an operator who knows the meter is watching: how much
%compute was run, and was it training?\par}
%\vspace{0.9cm}
%{\color{matsmaroon}\rule{\linewidth}{2pt}\par}

% --- MOTIVATION --------------------------------------------------------------
\postersection{Motivation}
Compute-governance agreements require \textit{verification}: an outside party must be
able to check what an operator actually ran, against an operator who may
cheat.

Hardware mechanisms for verification typically rely on reading silicon
the operator controls.

Off-chip analogue sensors (e.g. a power meter) have been identified as a
potential verification channel that is beyond the operator's reach.

Prior work fits forward models under cooperative conditions --- we ask
the inverse, adversarial question along the channel's two axes: total
\textbf{energy} (how much compute?) and \textbf{time structure} (was it
training?).

% --- ENERGY CHANNEL ----------------------------------------------------------
\postersection{Energy channel: counting operations}
Chip power is affine in the operation rate, $P = r\,F + P_0$, and the
exchange rate $r$ [J/op] is assembled from four physical factors ---
every one chosen by the operator, invisible to the meter:

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{exchange_scales}
\end{center}
\postercaption{Figure 1. The exchange rate, from transistors to the
powered die: operand toggling $\alpha$, number format $\kappa$, memory
locality $C_{\mathrm{eff}}$, voltage $V^2$; the clock cancels, and static
draw leaves as the overhead $P_0$.}

\vspace{0.25cm}
On an A100 these factors span a measured $24.8\times$, so a trace
identifies a \emph{set} of workloads, not a number. We derive the set's
width in closed form --- the floor $\beta$, the largest covert computation
the trace cannot rule out, in machines of declared capacity --- and
measure every input.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.59\linewidth}
\bulletitem{Explicit cheating strategies hide \textbf{0.41 machines} of
covert compute behind a trace the meter accepts.}
\bulletitem{Worst case: $\beta^{*} = \textbf{1.16}$ with the declared
number format checked; $\textbf{1.95}$ against a bare meter.}
\bulletitem{``Power plus paperwork'' stalls at $\beta^{*} \approx 1$;
re-execution at an observed operating point buys $\textbf{0.34}$.}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.37\linewidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{capability_ladder}
\end{minipage}
\postercaption{Figure 2. The capability ladder: worst-case covert compute
$\beta^{*}$ (machines of declared capacity) as the verifier gains
capabilities. The dashed line marks one whole machine of conceded
ambiguity.}

% --- TIME CHANNEL ------------------------------------------------------------
\postersection{Time channel: detecting training}
Synchronous training alternates compute and communication at an iteration
cadence of 0.5--1.5\,Hz, and a tracked cyclostationary detector recovers
this line at \textbf{AUC 1.00} against a hard inference null. The contest
is asymmetric: varying real work per iteration hides training from every
fixed-frequency test at \textbf{zero measured throughput cost}, yet the
tracking detector holds at detection rate 1.0 --- the best anchored attack
buys hiding of only \textbf{0.16} at \textbf{159\%} overhead.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\noindent
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.58\linewidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{st2_cost_pareto}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.38\linewidth}
\postercaption{Figure 3. The cost of hiding, priced with measured A100
throughput anchors: essentially free against fixed-frequency tests, but
the staircase never leaves the floor against the tracking class.}
\vspace{0.5cm}
\bulletitem{Minimum meter spec: $\geq 2$\,Hz sampling, $\leq 0.5$\,s
integration --- a 1\,s-averaging, 1\,Hz meter defeats every detector on an
honest workload.}
\end{minipage}

% --- LIMITATIONS -------------------------------------------------------------
\postersection{Limitations}
The energy floor is measured on one GPU model with the voltage axis
unswept, and it prices a cheating corner, so it upper-bounds the set
width. The detection results
rest on a literature-parameterised scenario model: on a measured A100 the
cadence modulates board power by only ${\sim}0.4\%$, some $75\times$
shallower than the model assumes. And the meter certifies a physical
schedule, not semantics: a decoy that computes gradients and discards them
scores as training under every rule.

% --- IMPLICATIONS ------------------------------------------------------------
\postersection{Implications}
The meter is an anchor, not an instrument: the one channel whose integrity
requires no trust in the operator --- a cheap, deployable front line that
prices the cheap evasions, not a proof of compliance. Each rung of the
capability ladder is a protocol requirement a treaty could stipulate; the
meter specification is procurement guidance. Escaping the ceilings means
leaving the passive channel: active challenge protocols and multi-channel
sensing are future work.

